// @run
// @log 7
// @log 7
// @log a
// @log a
// THE @log LINES ABOVE ARE THE WRONG ANSWERS. They are pinned so that fixing this is LOUD.
// Correct output is `99 / 7 / inner / a`.
//
// A local declaration that SHADOWS A PARAMETER is silently ignored: reads inside the inner
// scope resolve to the PARAMETER, not to the local just declared. `vl check --codegen` exits 0,
// the module validates, it runs, and it computes the wrong value.
//
// NO LITERAL UNIONS, no niches, no generics, and no annotation — a bare `const v = 99` over a
// parameter `v: i32` is enough (the annotated form reproduces identically). This
// is ordinary code, and it is the quietest failure class this corpus holds: not a reject, not
// invalid wasm, just a wrong number.
//
// IT CONTRADICTS THE STATED RULE. ROADMAP B16 records the current semantics as "same-scope
// redeclaration errors; nested shadowing allowed (uniquified in codegen)". Shadowing a
// PARAMETER is not uniquified — so either the uniquifier misses this case, or the checker should
// be rejecting the declaration. It does neither.
//
// THE NEIGHBOURING CASE WORKS, which is what localizes it: a local shadowing another LOCAL
// resolves correctly — see `tests/cases/literal-unions/shadowed-atom-widen-reserves-frame.vl`,
// which depends on it. Only a local shadowing a PARAMETER is lost.
//
// A SECOND SYMPTOM, same cause. When the shadowing local is a LITERAL UNION and its value is
// pushed into a `string[]`, the emitter refuses outright:
//
//   function f(v: i32, n: i32) {
//     const xs: string[] = []
//     if n > 0 { const v: K = "kb"  xs.push(v) }   // emitProgram: literal-union string
//   }                                              // widening with no members
//
// The widen fires (the value IS an atom) and then asks the node for its member texts — and the
// node it resolves is the PARAMETER's, an `i32` with no members.
//
// THERE ARE THREE SYMPTOMS, and the third is the one that matters most. Where the shadowed
// parameter and the shadowing local have COMPATIBLE reps it is a wrong VALUE (the `7 7` / `a a`
// pinned here). Where a literal union is involved it is a hard emit refusal (above). Where the
// types simply DIFFER — no literal union anywhere — it is `vl check --codegen` rc 0 plus an
// invalid module: `const v = "inner"` over a parameter `v: i32`, or `const v = 99` over
// `v: string`, both give `expected (ref $type), found i32`.
//
// AND THE CHECKER IS RIGHT WHILE CODEGEN IS WRONG, which makes this a checker/codegen TYPE
// CONFUSION rather than only a wrong number. `vl check` on the first of those emits
// `redundant type annotation: 'v' is inferred as 'string'` — the checker binds the inner name at
// its OWN type. Codegen does not: `localIndexOf` resolves PARAM-first while `exprIsLitAtom`'s
// Ident arm resolves DECLARED-LOCAL-first, so the two disagree about which binding a name means.
// SAME-SCOPE redeclaration of a parameter IS rejected (`redeclared v`), so only nested shadowing
// is silently accepted.
//
// All three reproduce identically before and after the reservation fix shipped alongside this
// pin, which is what says they are this defect and not that one.
//
// FOUND while fixing an unrelated defect — the reservation scan and the emit resolving a
// shadowed name to different bindings, which produced an unreserved scratch frame
// (`xfail-miscompile-shadowed-atom-widen-unreserved-frame.vl`, now closed). That fix made the
// reservation agree with the emit; this file records that the EMIT ITSELF resolves a
// param-shadowing local wrongly, which the reservation work did not touch and does not depend
// on.
function fInt(v: i32, n: i32) {
  if n > 0 {
    const v = 99
    print(v)
  }
  print(v)
}

function fStr(s: string, n: i32) {
  if n > 0 {
    const s = "inner"
    print(s)
  }
  print(s)
}

function main() {
  fInt(7, 1)
  fStr("a", 1)
}
main()
